Agent mail, an ngterm extension: hand the pane's `msg` mailbox to the coding agent running in it, so a message another
program leaves with `ngtermctl msg send` reaches the agent without anyone typing into its composer. A new adapter,
`ngterm-agent-mail.sh` (and its PowerShell form `ngterm-agent-mail.ps1`), runs `ngtermctl agent mail claude` for Claude
Code's PostToolUse (the mail as added context) and, with --wait, as the asyncRewake waiter on SessionStart and Stop that
wakes an idle agent, turning the pane's status back to active when it does. The Codex adapter runs
`ngtermctl agent mail codex` on UserPromptSubmit, PostToolUse and Stop and prints its hook answer for Codex (a Stop
that hands mail over keeps the row active and the watcher running), reading a PostToolUse or Stop payload only when the
pane has an id; it skips the memory-consolidation thread and a `codex exec`, `review`, `app-server` or `mcp-server`
parent. Applied after 0006-agent-transcript.patch.

diff --git a/ngterm-agent-mail.ps1 b/ngterm-agent-mail.ps1
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..342ed48a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/ngterm-agent-mail.ps1
@@ -0,0 +1,48 @@
+# ngterm-agent-mail — hand the pane's mail to the Claude Code that runs in it (Windows).
+#
+#   powershell -NoProfile -NonInteractive -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File ngterm-agent-mail.ps1 claude          # PostToolUse
+#   powershell -NoProfile -NonInteractive -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File ngterm-agent-mail.ps1 claude --wait   # SessionStart, Stop (asyncRewake)
+#
+# The Windows form of ngterm-agent-mail.sh, for Windows PowerShell 5.1 and PowerShell 7; its header holds what the
+# hooks do. The payload is read and handed on as UTF-8 whatever the console's code page, `ngtermctl agent mail` writes
+# the hook's answer straight to the streams Claude Code reads, and the exit status is the CLI's: 2 when --wait took
+# mail, which also turns the pane's status back to active. Outside ngterm it does nothing and exits 0.
+#
+# ngtermctl resolution: $env:NGTERMCTL, else the path the installer bakes in ($NgtermctlBaked), else `ngtermctl` on
+# PATH.
+
+if (-not $env:NGTERM_SESSION_ID -or -not $env:NGTERM_PANE_ID) { exit 0 }
+
+$statusWrapper = $env:NGTERM_STATUS_WRAPPER
+if (-not $statusWrapper) { $statusWrapper = Join-Path $PSScriptRoot 'ngterm-agent-status.ps1' }
+
+$ngtermctl = 'ngtermctl'
+if ($env:NGTERMCTL) {
+  $ngtermctl = $env:NGTERMCTL
+} elseif ((Get-Variable -Name NgtermctlBaked -ValueOnly -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)) {
+  $ngtermctl = $NgtermctlBaked
+}
+
+$payload = ''
+if ([Console]::IsInputRedirected) {
+  try {
+    $stdin = [System.IO.StreamReader]::new([Console]::OpenStandardInput(), [System.Text.UTF8Encoding]::new($false))
+    $payload = $stdin.ReadToEnd()
+  } catch { }
+}
+if (-not $payload) { exit 0 }
+
+$cli = @('agent', 'mail') + @($args)
+if ($env:NGTERM_SOCKET) { $cli += @('--socket', $env:NGTERM_SOCKET) }
+
+$status = 1
+try {
+  # a `New-Object` encoding is wrapped, which Windows PowerShell 5.1 does not take for a native command's input
+  $OutputEncoding = [System.Text.UTF8Encoding]::new($false)
+  $payload | & $ngtermctl @cli
+  $status = $LASTEXITCODE
+} catch { }
+if ($status -eq 2) {
+  try { & $statusWrapper active --blink *> $null } catch { }
+}
+exit $status
diff --git a/ngterm-agent-mail.sh b/ngterm-agent-mail.sh
new file mode 100755
index 00000000..c39baea7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/ngterm-agent-mail.sh
@@ -0,0 +1,38 @@
+#!/usr/bin/env bash
+# ngterm-agent-mail — hand the pane's mail to the Claude Code that runs in it.
+#
+#   ngterm-agent-mail.sh claude          # Claude Code PostToolUse: the mail as added context
+#   ngterm-agent-mail.sh claude --wait   # Claude Code SessionStart and Stop, asyncRewake: wake an idle agent
+#
+# Installed by ngterm's Help ▸ Install Agent Status Hooks command. An ngterm extension beyond upstream, whose agents
+# read their mailbox (`ngtermctl msg read`) only when they decide to.
+#
+# Another program leaves a message in this pane's mailbox with `ngtermctl msg send --to SESSION:PANE`; this hands it to
+# the pane's agent, so a dispatcher can steer a worker session, or a worker report back, without typing into a
+# composer. The work is `ngtermctl agent mail claude`, which reads the hook payload on stdin, takes the mailbox's
+# messages in one read+ack and prints the hook's answer: added context on PostToolUse, and with --wait it waits in the
+# background, then prints the mail on stderr and exits 2, which wakes an idle Claude Code with it. Only the pane's
+# interactive agent takes mail: a subagent's hook and a `claude -p` worker (which inherits the pane's NGTERM_*) take
+# none. A wake also turns the pane's status back to active, since a woken turn fires no UserPromptSubmit.
+#
+# Its stdout and stderr are the hook's answer, so it adds nothing of its own; outside ngterm (NGTERM_SESSION_ID or
+# NGTERM_PANE_ID unset) it does nothing and exits 0. Codex's mail goes through its lifecycle adapter instead, whose
+# hook lines Codex has already trusted.
+#
+# ngtermctl resolution: $NGTERMCTL, else the path the installer bakes in, else `ngtermctl` on PATH.
+set -u
+
+[ -n "${NGTERM_SESSION_ID:-}" ] && [ -n "${NGTERM_PANE_ID:-}" ] || exit 0
+
+script_dir=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")" 2>/dev/null && pwd)
+status_wrapper=${NGTERM_STATUS_WRAPPER:-"$script_dir/ngterm-agent-status.sh"}
+
+args=(agent mail "$@")
+[ -n "${NGTERM_SOCKET:-}" ] && args+=(--socket "$NGTERM_SOCKET")
+
+"${NGTERMCTL:-ngtermctl}" "${args[@]}"
+status=$?
+if [ "$status" -eq 2 ]; then
+  "$status_wrapper" active --blink >/dev/null 2>&1 </dev/null || true
+fi
+exit "$status"
